# The Weave

Everything you have read so far is loose: a folder of `.work` files, each a
unit in its own language — Elixir flows and compute on the [[Pipeline]] and
[[Score]] side, a Svelte [[chart]] and a SolidJS [[sparkline]] for the
dashboard, a Rust+Zig [[forecast]] kernel, a Python [[risk]] unit. The
**weave** is the step that takes that whole tree and produces ONE shippable
thing: `salesapp.html`.

This file is where the format closes the loop on itself. The command is
`work bundle`. It does three jobs in order — resolve every reference, audit
every capability and dependency, run every test — and only then does it emit
the bundle. A red test ships no bundle. That gate is not a lint pass bolted on
the side; it is the same [[code graph]] the whole workbook is built from,
walked one last time with the build's eyes open.

## What "polyglot" actually means here

The Sales workbook is written in five languages and the weave does not flatten
them into one. It places each unit where its **placement** says it runs, in the
form its language compiles to. There are only ever two placements — `client`
and `server` — and the weave's job is to honor them across the language mix:

- **client islands** — the [[chart]] (Svelte) and the [[sparkline]] (SolidJS)
  are browser units. They compile to wasm + a small JS shim and **hydrate** in
  the page: the bundle ships their rendered HTML for first paint, then the
  island wakes up and takes over its own subtree. Two different frameworks,
  side by side, in the same document.
- **server compute as sandboxed wasm** — the [[forecast]] unit (Rust
  orchestrating a Zig numeric kernel) and the [[risk]] unit (Python) are
  foreign languages placed on the **server**. On the server a foreign-language
  unit runs as a **sandboxed wasm component** beside the BEAM, behind the same
  grant-by-construction wall the [[Pricing]] chapter described.
- **server Elixir runs native** — [[Pipeline]], [[Score]], [[Enrich]] are
  native Elixir on the BEAM. Real OTP, not wasm. The trusted tier. The weave
  links them as the host the wasm components call back into.

The thing to hold onto: the weave never says "server runs as wasm." Server
Elixir is native; foreign server units are wasm *beside* it; client units are
wasm *in the browser*. One bundle, three execution shapes, one authoring model.

## Mixed file types compose — Svelte and SolidJS together

The dashboard region pulls two client islands from two different frameworks
into the same parent. Neither knows the other exists; the weave hydrates each
against its own runtime and lets them share the page. Here is a region that
mounts the [[chart]] beside the [[sparkline]]:

client svelte :score_panel do
  import { chart } from "work://chart"
  import { sparkline } from "work://sparkline"

  // both islands receive the same ranked %Lead{} list the :pipeline produced;
  // chart draws the bars, sparkline draws the 30-day trend. Two frameworks,
  // one data feed, one hydrated subtree.
  export function ScorePanel(leads) {
    return panel(
      chart({ leads: leads, x: "name", y: "score" }),
      sparkline({ series: leads.map(l => l.score), stroke: "currentColor" })
    )
  }
end

The weave sees `work://chart` resolve to a `svelte` unit and `work://sparkline`
resolve to a `solid` unit. It compiles each with its own toolchain, emits two
hydration bundles, and stitches their first-paint HTML into the region. At
runtime the page carries a Svelte island and a Solid island in the same DOM —
no shared framework runtime forced on either, because each island brings only
what it needs.

## Mixed file types compose — Rust and Zig together

[[forecast]] is one unit made of two languages. Rust owns the orchestration —
shaping `%Lead{}` revenue history into an input slice, calling the kernel,
handing back a `{:ok, projection}` variant. Zig owns the hot loop: the actual
projection loop, written tight against raw memory. They compile into ONE
wasm component; the weave links the Zig object into the Rust unit before it
ever becomes a sandbox citizen:

rust :forecast, in: :forecast do
  // Rust orchestrates. The Zig kernel is linked in as an extern; from the
  // unit's contract it is just another function — the language seam vanishes
  // once both halves are wasm in the same component.
  extern "C" {
    fn project_series(base: *const f64, len: usize, periods: usize, growth: f64, out: *mut f64);
  }

  pub fn project(history: &[f64], periods: usize, growth: f64) -> Result<Vec<f64>, String> {
    if history.len() < 3 {
      return Err("need at least three points to project".into());
    }
    let mut out = vec![0.0_f64; periods];
    unsafe {
      project_series(history.as_ptr(), history.len(), periods, growth, out.as_mut_ptr());
    }
    Ok(out)
  }
end

And the Zig kernel that Rust links against — pure numerics, no capabilities,
the kind of code you want compiled to the tightest wasm possible:

zig :kernel, in: :forecast do
  // The heavy math. Projects `periods` future points from the last known
  // value at a fixed growth rate. No allocation, no grants — it only ever
  // touches the buffers Rust hands it across the linear-memory boundary.
  export fn project_series(base: [*]const f64, len: usize, periods: usize, growth: f64, out: [*]f64) void {
    const last = base[len - 1];
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < periods) : (i += 1) {
      const step: f64 = @floatFromInt(i + 1);
      out[i] = last * std.math.pow(f64, 1.0 + growth, step);
    }
  }
end

The weave compiles Zig to a wasm object, compiles Rust to a wasm object,
links them into one component, then drops that component into the `:forecast`
sandbox. From the [[code graph]] there is a single `:forecast` node with an
internal language edge to `:kernel`; from the bundle there is one
sandboxed wasm unit. Two languages, one citizen.

## Mixed file types compose — Python alongside

[[risk]] is a third language again — Python — and it sits beside the Rust+Zig
forecast in the same delivery without any of them touching the others. It is a
server compute unit, so the weave compiles it to a sandboxed wasm component and
grants it nothing it did not ask for:

python :risk, in: :risk_box do
  # A risk score from lead features. Pure function of the struct's fields, so
  # it needs no net, no kv — the weave gives it an empty grant by construction.
  def risk(lead):
    churn = 0.4 if lead["employees"] < 10 else 0.1
    thin = 0.3 if lead["revenue"] < 20_000 else 0.0
    stale = 0.2 if lead["status"] == "new" else 0.0
    raw = churn + thin + stale
    return min(round(raw * 100), 100)  # 0..100, higher = riskier
end

So in the final `salesapp.html` you have, simultaneously: a Svelte island and a
Solid island hydrating in the browser; a Rust+Zig component and a Python
component running as sandboxed wasm beside the BEAM; and native Elixir flows
orchestrating all of it. Five languages, three execution shapes, one file. None
of them was rewritten to get along — the weave honored each placement and let
the [[Dock]] broker the seams between them.

## The weave gate — three jobs, in order

`work bundle` does not blindly concatenate. It runs the gate, and the gate is
the same three checks every time. If any one fails, no `salesapp.html` is
written.

**1. Resolve every reference.** Each `[[backlink]]`, each `work://` deep link,
each inline `:atom` and `@type` in prose, each cross-unit call, is resolved
against the workspace symbol table. A `work://sparkline` that points at nothing,
a [[forecast]] that calls a kernel function the Zig side never exported — these
are unresolved edges, and the gate stops on them. You cannot bundle a dangling
reference.

**2. Audit caps and deps.** The gate walks the capability edges. It checks that
every `grant:` is actually declared on the sandbox the unit joined, that no unit
reaches a host it was never granted (the [[risk]] unit asking for `:net` it
never requested would fail here), and that the dependency graph has no cycle a
delivery mode could not resolve. The forecast's `:forecast` sandbox, the risk
unit's `:risk_box` sandbox, the pricing wall from the previous chapter — all of
them are audited as code-graph nodes with audited edges, not trusted on faith.

**3. Run every test.** Then, and only then, the gate runs the tests attached to
the units — the [[Score]] arithmetic, the [[Pipeline]] ranking, the forecast
fit, the risk bounds. This is the hard rule: **a red test ships no bundle.** The
gate is where correctness becomes a precondition of delivery instead of a thing
you hope someone checked.

server task :weave_gate do
  @moduledoc "What `work bundle` runs before it writes salesapp.html. Any :error halts the build."

  def run(tree) do
    with {:ok, graph} <- resolve_refs(tree),     # job 1: no dangling [[..]] / work:// / :atom
         {:ok, _audit} <- audit_caps(graph),     # job 2: every grant declared, no ungranted reach
         {:ok, report} <- run_tests(graph) do     # job 3: red test ships no bundle
      {:ok, emit_bundle(graph, report)}           # -> salesapp.html, gzipped, self-contained
    else
      {:error, reason} -> {:halt, reason}          # no file written
    end
  end
end

The result of a green run is a single gzipped, self-contained `salesapp.html`:
the page, plus a compressed blob of the whole tree hydrated client-side, plus
the wasm components the server tier loads. That is the `wbundle-html/1` format —
the same one the [[Welcome]] chapter named at the very start.

## One bundle, three delivery modes

The weave emits the same artifact no matter how you intend to serve it; the
delivery mode is a *runtime* choice, not a rebuild:

- **static bundle** — open `salesapp.html` anywhere. The client islands
  ([[chart]], [[sparkline]]) hydrate; the server units are dormant until a
  nexus is reachable. No backend required to read the workbook.
- **nexus SSR** — served by the nexus, the server regions ([[Pipeline]],
  [[Score]], and the sandboxed [[forecast]]/[[risk]] wasm) render on the BEAM
  and ship HTML; the client units stay islands. Same file, server-rendered.
- **live SSR** — over the RCP websocket, the server diffs region state and
  pushes patches. The [[Dashboard]] updates as the [[Pipeline]] reranks, with
  no client rebuild.

You authored once. The weave bundled once. The same `salesapp.html` is a static
page, an SSR app, and a live app depending only on whether — and how — a nexus
is on the other end. That is the payoff of putting placement, sandboxing, and
the code graph in the authoring model instead of the build script: the build
has nothing left to decide. It resolves, audits, tests, and weaves — and ships
the workbook you wrote.
